# M13 — the CREATEROLE predicate made ALWAYS TRUE (opposite polarity to M9).
#
# M9..M12 each delete a predicate and prove a control notices its absence. That family
# cannot detect the opposite failure: a predicate that matches EVERYTHING. An
# always-matching predicate makes the guard refuse connections for reasons that are not
# true of them, and — worse for the suite — it makes every "the message names this reason"
# assertion in role-guard-predicates.test.ts pass WITHOUT its carrier mutation. Those four
# controls would then be measuring nothing, while still reporting green.
#
# The catcher is the `baseline` test, whose whole job is to assert that with NO carrier
# applied, none of the four new reason codes is reported. It is the control on the controls,
# and this mutation is what shows it has teeth.
#
# Note the positive control is NOT expected to fail here: statesman_app reaches no role at
# all, so the reachability filter keeps its verdict clean however permissive the privilege
# predicate becomes. That asymmetry is the point of having both tests.
id: M13
title: privileged-role's CREATEROLE reason is ORed with true, so every role matches it
expect: caught
proves: the `baseline` test keeps the four reason-code assertions honest — a predicate that matches everything is caught, not just one that matches nothing
expect-test: baseline: statesman_migrator is refused for OWNERSHIP ONLY

file: src/server/db/privileged-role.ts
count: 1
@@FIND
    sql: (a) => `${a}.rolcreaterole`,
@@REPLACE
    sql: (a) => `(${a}.rolcreaterole or true)`,
@@END
